ungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/2503191"} "Kristóf Milák"] " had won silver at Tokyo 2020 and took gold in the 200 fly. He won this event at the 2022 World Championships and the 2020 and 2022 European Championships, and was the favorite in Paris. Three days earlier, he had taken silver in the 200 butterfly."] "\n\n" [:p {} "His biggest challengers in the final were expected to be Swiss " [:a {:href "/athletes/2504016"} "Noè Ponti"] ", bronze medalist at Tokyo 2020, Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/140836"} "Matthew Temple"] ", and Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/2502711"} "Josh Liendo"] ", medalist at the 2022 (bronze) and 2023 (silver) World Championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final was led early by Dutch swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/145597"} "Nyls Korstanje"] ", but he flagged badly on the final lap and finished sixth. Temple was never in it and finished just behind him. Liendo was second at the turn, but Milák would finish in 26.50 to take gold in 49.90, his second fastest time ever, with Liendo taking silver, also under 50 seconds in 49.99, only the fifth swimmer to break that barrier. Liendo also became the first Black Canadian swimmer to win an Olympic medal. Third place would go to another Canadian, " [:a {:href "/athletes/154417"} "Ilya Kharun"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2503191"} "Kristóf Milák"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "50.19 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "50.38 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "49.90 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2502711"} "Josh Liendo"]] [:td {} [:a {